{"t": 630.248, "kind": "tool_call", "tool": "update_finding", "args": {"finding_id": "f_d73014df", "description": "Bulk extractor email artifacts recovered from the disk image contain a reference to \"ALL CARDERS\" in what appears to be a Usenet newsgroup message or email list. The recovered data shows an email address c.sharpe@earthlink.net signed as \"Chucky\" in the same message thread. Additionally, Mr. Evil user's recent documents include \"Receipt.rtf\" (accessed 2004-08-20 15:09:16). However, this evidence represents only fragmentary references to carder networks in email/newsgroup content - the actual mailbox/message content has not been analyzed. The presence of packet sniffing tools (Ethereal, tcpdump, Cain & Abel) suggests potential capability for credential interception, but no evidence of actual successful credential capture, credit card theft, or financial fraud transactions has been identified. The mention of \"$3500 weekly\" cannot be verified as related to fraudulent activity without more complete email context. This finding indicates possible interest in or subscription to carder networks, but does not constitute confirmed evidence of active credit card fraud activity.", "severity": "medium", "confidence": "inference"}, "is_error": false, "result": "[mulder audit: output blake2b:55ffb49f92f46586504022b2ead376d33049e205cfcc753f93a06de4965536cf, 18 ms]"}
{"t": 636.344, "kind": "tool_call", "tool": "update_finding", "args": {"finding_id": "f_cdcd6625", "description": "The system contains multiple packet capture and network analysis tools, all executed by the \"Mr. Evil\" user account. Evidence includes: (1) WinPcap 3.01 installer executed from Mr. Evil's desktop on 2004-08-27 15:15:08; (2) Ethereal (Wireshark predecessor) setup 0.10.6 executed from desktop on 2004-08-27 15:28:36, followed by Ethereal.exe execution at 15:34:54; (3) Cain & Abel password recovery/packet sniffing tool executed on 2004-08-27 15:33:02 and installed on 2004-08-20 15:05:52; (4) Look@LAN network monitoring tool executed 2004-08-26 15:06:14. These tools are commonly used for network eavesdropping and password interception. HOWEVER: Recovered packet data (bulk.packets) shows only normal outbound HTTP traffic from the system to legitimate websites (Microsoft, etc.) and UPnP discovery broadcasts from the local router (192.168.254.254). No evidence of actual packet captures from other systems, credential interception, or eavesdropping activity has been found in the available packet data. NOTE: No .pcap packet capture files were found in the file system despite tool presence. The tools were installed and executed, but evidence of successful packet capture and credential extraction remains unverified.", "confidence": "inference"}, "is_error": false, "result": "[mulder audit: output blake2b:9b3124a4a74336243deaba68dabbf8b04577b536252184f8c534b4efc003024a, 19 ms]"}
{"t": 641.297, "kind": "tool_call", "tool": "update_finding", "args": {"finding_id": "f_a9b10ff1", "description": "The \"Mr. Evil\" user executed mIRC (Internet Relay Chat client) on 2004-08-25 16:20:27 and 2004-08-20 15:09:46 (earlier version). Bulk extractor domain data recovered from the disk image shows fragmentary references to IRC networks including undernet.org and IRCnet, with mentions of various channel topics and email contacts. Additionally, Anonymizer software installation from 2004-08-20 15:05:00 indicates intent to mask communications and network connections. However, the evidence consists only of extracted domain names and email references embedded in recovered data - no actual IRC chat logs, conversation content, or coordination messages have been retrieved and analyzed. The presence of mIRC combined with anonymous remailers suggests capability for covert communication, but without access to actual IRC channel conversations or evidence of specific attack coordination, the nature and extent of IRC activity remains undetermined.", "confidence": "inference"}, "is_error": false, "result": "[mulder audit: output blake2b:f66909e79c9f08bf107b3234d9240a8c093c96092ec49531adb02c87fceb05df, 33 ms]"}
{"t": 645.775, "kind": "tool_call", "tool": "update_finding", "args": {"finding_id": "f_f413badf", "description": "The Mr. Evil user account contains an Outlook Express mailbox file named \"alt.hacking.dbx\" (file path: Documents and Settings/Mr. Evil/Local Settings/Application Data/Identities/.../Microsoft/Outlook Express/alt.hacking.dbx). The \\\"alt.hacking\\\" reference refers to the Usenet newsgroup alt.hacking, which is a forum for discussing hacking techniques. This file name indicates the user subscribed to this newsgroup and maintained a local copy of messages. However, the mailbox file contents have not been extracted or analyzed - only the filename presence has been identified. While subscription to hacking-related newsgroups indicates interest in hacking topics, the actual mailbox content (if any), the nature of messages, and whether they contain attack planning or coordination instructions remain unknown. The presence of this mailbox alongside other hacking tools suggests potential engagement with hacker communities, but does not constitute evidence of receiving attack coordination or planning messages without content analysis.", "confidence": "inference"}, "is_error": false, "result": "[mulder audit: output blake2b:83a87f63c95e5202886175fb49a5eb5a045d6d807f572ce78d51d8e7e9df725d, 16 ms]"}
